#### uses scalar qualified symbol indexes for staged lookup

- Verify: uses scalar qualified symbol indexes for staged lookup


<details>
<summary>Executable SSpec</summary>

Runnable source: 25 lines folded for reproduction.
Reproduction: this block contains the complete executable scenario source.

```simple
# @req: REQ-SSPEC-LOCAL-001
step("Verify: uses scalar qualified symbol indexes for staged lookup")
# QTYPEIDX (2026-08-22): this row used to freeze the parallel-array
# form. That form made every qualified lookup O(bindings) with two text
# comparisons per row, and 84% of the probes are MISSES, which always
# pay the full length -- `callable_deps` became the largest exclusive
# term in the whole HIR phase. The ban was on the `.has(key)` guard
# shape, which is still banned below; the index itself is now the
# `contains_key` Dict shape every other 2026-08-21 index on this path
# already uses (PKGDEP, PKGIDX, IMPLIDX, MODCALLIDX). The lookups still
# return a raw scalar `i64`, which is the actual staged-native contract.
# doc/08_tracking/bug/hir_qualified_type_lookup_linear_scan_2026-08-22.md
val symbols = rt_file_read_text("src/compiler/20.hir/hir_types.spl") ?? ""
expect(symbols).to_contain("qualified_types: Dict<text, i64>")
expect(symbols).to_contain("qualified_functions: Dict<text, i64>")
expect(symbols).to_contain("fn lookup_qualified_type_raw(module_name: text, member_name: text) -> i64:")
expect(symbols).to_contain("fn lookup_qualified_function_raw(module_name: text, member_name: text) -> i64:")
# The index key must stay injective: `.` aliases ("a.b","c") onto
# ("a","b.c"); `#` cannot occur in a module path or a member name.
expect(symbols).to_contain("module_name + \"#\" + member_name")
# No return to the linear parallel-array scan.
expect(symbols.contains("qualified_type_module_names")).to_be(false)
expect(symbols.contains("qualified_function_names")).to_be(false)
expect(symbols.contains("if self.qualified_types.has(key):")).to_be(false)
expect(symbols.contains("if self.qualified_functions.has(key):")).to_be(false)
```

</details>

